(Abstract) Tj
T*
(People who inject drugs \(PWID\) experience disproportionate hepatitis C burden but) Tj
T*
(encounter substantial barriers to accessing curative treatment, including fragmented) Tj
T*
(healthcare systems, geographic distance, and stigma. This protocol describes a randomized) Tj
T*
(trial evaluating an accessible care model wherein hepatitis C screening, treatment) Tj
T*
(initiation, and clinical support are delivered directly on-site at harm-reduction) Tj
T*
(facilities, compared with standard referral-based pathways. An estimated 165 HCV RNA-) Tj
T*
(positive PWID reporting injection drug use within the prior 90 days will be randomized 1:1) Tj
T*
(to accessible on-site care or standard referral. Primary outcome measures assess) Tj
T*
(treatment-related changes in injection frequency, injection-related risk behaviors, and) Tj
T*
(uptake of opioid-use-disorder treatment during 12 months of follow-up. Secondary outcomes) Tj
T*
(examine hepatitis C treatment initiation and completion rates. This pragmatic design tests) Tj
T*
(whether eliminating geographic, administrative, and stigma-related barriers to hepatitis C) Tj
T*
(care influences both health-related behaviors and disease treatment engagement in an) Tj
T*
(underserved population.) Tj
